%[text] %[text:anchor:T_89C495FF] # RLC Circuits Lab
%[text] [⇦ DC Circuit Analysis Overview](<matlab: OpenOverview>)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9E1B6A58] ## Introduction
%[text] In the example script, [RLCCircuits.m](<matlab:open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'Scripts','RLCCircuits.m'))>), a series RLC circuit was analyzed. Altering the topology of the RLC circuit will affect its behavior and mathematical analysis. In this lab, you will analyze the RLC circuit shown below.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![psRLCcircuit.png](text:image:858d)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *An RLC circuit*
%[text] For this section, assume that the circuit has been charged by $V${"altText":"V"} for a long time. Then, at time $t = 0${"altText":"t = 0"}, the switch moves to position 2 (the discharging configuration).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FA5F8C3C] ## Part 1: ODE Analysis
%[text] In this section, you will derive an ODE that reflects the circuit behavior and solve for the damping factor. 
%[text] **Task 1.** Derive the second order ODE for the voltage across the capacitor $V\_C${"altText":"V\_C"} in the illustrated RLC circuit. 
%[text] **Hints**: 
%[text] 1. Apply KCL to relate the currents through the circuit elements.
%[text] 2. Apply KVL around a closed loop that includes the inductor to relate the inductor voltage to the capacitor voltage.
%[text] 3. Write the equation in terms of the capacitor voltage using the three fundamental relationships listed below. \
%[text]{"align":"center"} $i\_C = C \\frac{dV\_c}{dt}${"altText":"i\_C = C \frac{dV\_c}{dt}"} $V\_R = i\_R R${"altText":"V\_R = i\_R R"} $V\_L = L \\frac{di\_L}{dt}${"altText":"V\_L = L \frac{di\_L}{dt}"}
%[text] **Task 2.** Rewrite the second order ODE in the form
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{d^2 V\_C}{dt^2} + 2 \\alpha \\frac{dV\_C}{dt} + {\\omega\_0}^2 V\_C= 0${"altText":"\frac{d^2 V\_C}{dt^2} + 2 \alpha \frac{dV\_C}{dt} + {\omega\_0}^2 V\_C= 0"}
%[text] **Solution:** $\\frac{d^2 V\_C}{dt^2} +\\frac{1}{RC} \\frac{dV\_C}{dt} + \\frac{1}{LC} V\_C= 0${"altText":"\frac{d^2 V\_C}{dt^2} +\frac{1}{RC} \frac{dV\_C}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC} V\_C= 0"}
%[text] **Task 3**. What are the values of $\\alpha${"altText":"\alpha"} and $\\omega\_0${"altText":"\omega\_0"} in terms of $R${"altText":"R"}, $L${"altText":"L"}, and $C${"altText":"C"}? Write your answers in the space provided below.
syms R L C % Symbolic variables for you to use
% Write your answer in terms of R, L, and C
alpha = 1/(2*R*C)
omega0 = 1/sqrt(L*C)
%[text] **Task 4.** What is the damping factor of the circuit, $\\zeta${"altText":"\zeta"}?
%[text] Recall that $\\zeta = \\frac{\\alpha}{\\omega\_0}${"altText":"\zeta = \frac{\alpha}{\omega\_0}"}.
% Record the expression for the damping factor
zeta = 1/(2*R)*sqrt(L/C)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_06D1A3BD] ## Part 2: Measurement
%[text] In this section, you will select component values to meet design criteria and then test the circuit using the virtual oscilloscope.
%[text] **Task 5.** Identify values of R, L, and C that satisfy the criteria outlined below.
%[text] 1. The circuit uses a 10 mH inductor.
%[text] 2. The circuit has a resonant frequency $\\omega\_0 = 2 \\pi (100)${"altText":"\omega\_0 = 2 \pi (100)"} rad/s.
%[text] 3. The circuit is underdamped with a damping factor $\\zeta = 0.5${"altText":"\zeta = 0.5"}. \
% Record the values of L, C, and R below. Use units of Henries, Farads, and Ohms.
L = 10e-3  
C = 1/(L*(2*pi*(100))^2)
R = 1/(2*0.5)*sqrt(L/C)
%[text] **Task 6.** Build the circuit and test it using the virtual oscilloscope (linked below).
%[text] - Set the element values to the ones you identified in **Task 5**.
%[text] - Ensure that the oscilloscope is on. 
%[text] - Use a square wave with a frequency of 20 Hz.
%[text] - Does the output of the system appear underdamped? \
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![image5554.png](text:image:3972)](<matlab: VirtualOscilloscope>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`VirtualOscilloscope.slx`](<matlab: VirtualOscilloscope>)
%[text] **Task 7.** Using the values for $L${"altText":"L"} and $C${"altText":"C"} you found in **Task 5**, what resistor will produce a critically damped system? 
% Record R here in Ohms
R_task7 = 1/(2*1)*sqrt(L/C)
%[text] **Task 8.** Test the circuit using the virtual oscilloscope with the value of $R${"altText":"R"} you identified in **Task 7.** Does the response appear critically damped?
%[text] **Task 9.** Using the values for $L${"altText":"L"} and $C${"altText":"C"} you found in **Task 5**, what resistor will produce an overdamped system with a damping factor of $\\zeta = 3${"altText":"\zeta = 3"}?
% Record R here in Ohms
R_task9 = 1/(2*3)*sqrt(L/C)
%[text] **Task 10.** Test the circuit using the virtual oscilloscope with the value of $R${"altText":"R"} you identified in **Task 9.** Does the response appear overdamped?
